  ],
  "text": "You have three colour receptors. A mantis shrimp has sixteen. Every colour you have ever seen is a mixture of three. A bee sees ultraviolet, which is a colour you have no name for. Flowers have landing stripes painted in it. Aimed at insects, invisible to you. And many birds look nothing to each other like they look to us. But more receptors does not simply mean better. That is the twist. The mantis shrimp does not see more colours. It sorts them much faster. So the strange part is not the number. It is that seeing has more than one design. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
